ll with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs to exactly one of the rectangles: if \\(i>j\\) it lies in \\(L_j\\); if \\(i<j\\) it lies in \\(R_i\\).  \nThe diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are left uncovered, satisfying the requirement that each row and each column have exactly one uncovered square.\n\nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left rectangles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right rectangles}}=2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nEquation (3) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles for any admissible placement.  \nEquation (4) exhibits a placement that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nHence the bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
