                "content": "**Solution Overview**\n\nLet  \n\n* the board be the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with  \n  \\(n=2025\\);\n* the uncovered squares be \\(\\{(i,\\pi (i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) where\n  \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (exactly one uncovered\n  square in each row and each column).\n\nAll other squares have to be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles,\neach tile being a set  \n\n\\[\nR=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\qquad (1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le d\\le n)\n\\]\n\nthat contains **no** point \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\nThe problem is therefore:\n\n> Find the smallest possible number of such rectangles that partition the\n> set \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq \\pi(i)\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.  A construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor every row \\(i\\) write  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) (resp. \\(R_i\\)) is the part of row \\(i\\) that lies left (resp.\nright) of the uncovered square.  \nIf \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i=\\varnothing\\); if \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) then\n\\(R_i=\\varnothing\\).\n\nEach non‑empty set \\(L_i\\) (resp. \\(R_i\\)) is a single horizontal\nsegment, i.e. a rectangle of height \\(1\\).  \nAll the sets \\(L_i\\) and \\(R_i\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together they\ncover every square that is not uncovered.\n\nBecause the column \\(1\\) occurs as \\(\\pi(i)\\) for exactly one row,\nexactly one of the \\(L_i\\) is empty; similarly exactly one of the\n\\(R_i\\) is empty. Consequently the total number of non‑empty sets is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\pi(i)>1\\}}+\n                    \\mathbf 1_{\\{\\pi(i)<n\\}}\\bigr)=\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the board can always be tiled with **\\(2n-2\\) tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  A lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n* the **leftmost covered square**  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  (i,\\pi(i)-1) &\\text{if }\\pi(i)>1,\\\\[2mm]\n  \\text{undefined} &\\text{if }\\pi(i)=1,\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n\n* the **rightmost covered square**  \n\n  \\[\n  r_i=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  (i,\\pi(i)+1) &\\text{if }\\pi(i)<n,\\\\[2mm]\n  \\text{undefined} &\\text{if }\\pi(i)=n .\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n\nHence we obtain exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{rows with }\\pi(i)>1}\n\\quad\\text{leftmost squares } \\ell_i,\n\\qquad\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{rows with }\\pi(i)<n}\n\\quad\\text{rightmost squares } r_i,\n\\]\n\ni.e. **\\(2n-2\\) distinct squares** of the board.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma  \n\n*No tile can contain two different leftmost squares \\(\\ell_i,\\ell_k\\)\n(\\(i\\neq k\\)).  \nThe same holds for the rightmost squares \\(r_i\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a tile \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) contains \\(\\ell_i\\) and\n\\(\\ell_k\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\(\\ell_k=(k,\\pi(k)-1)\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nc\\le \\pi(i)-1,\\qquad d\\ge \\pi(k)-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince the column \\(\\pi(i)\\) belongs to the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\)\nand \\(\\pi(i)\\) lies between \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) (because\n\\(c\\le\\pi(i)-1<\\pi(i)\\le d\\)), the tile would also contain the point\n\\((i,\\pi(i))\\) – a contradiction.  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause of the lemma, **each tile can contain at most one of the\n\\(2n-2\\) distinguished squares**.  Since every tile must contain at least\none of them (otherwise the tile would be completely surrounded by\nuncovered squares, which is impossible), any tiling of the board needs\nat least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Minimal number of tiles\n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with \