ssary  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n* the **leftmost covered square**  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  (i,\\pi(i)-1) &\\text{if }\\pi(i)>1,\\\\[2mm]\n  \\text{undefined} &\\text{if }\\pi(i)=1,\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n\n* the **rightmost covered square**  \n\n  \\[\n  r_i=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  (i,\\pi(i)+1) &\\text{if }\\pi(i)<n,\\\\[2mm]\n  \\text{undefined} &\\text{if }\\pi(i)=n .\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n\nHence we obtain exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{rows with }\\pi(i)>1}\n\\quad\\text{leftmost squares } \\ell_i,\n\\qquad\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{rows with }\\pi(i)<n}\n\\quad\\text{rightmost squares } r_i,\n\\]\n\ni.e. **\\(2n-2\\) distinct squares** of the board.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma  \n\n*No tile can contain two different leftmost squares \\(\\ell_i,\\ell_k\\)\n(\\(i\\neq k\\)).  \nThe same holds for the rightmost squares \\(r_i\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a tile \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) contains \\(\\ell_i\\) and\n\\(\\ell_k\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\(\\ell_k=(k,\\pi(k)-1)\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nc\\le \\pi(i)-1,\\qquad d\\ge \\pi(k)-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince the column \\(\\pi(i)\\) belongs to the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\)\nand \\(\\pi(i)\\) lies between \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) (because\n\\(c\\le\\pi(i)-1<\\pi(i)\\le d\\)), the tile would also contain the point\n\\((i,\\pi(i))\\) \u2013 a contradiction.  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause of the lemma, **each tile can contain at most one of the\n\\(2n-2\\) distinguished squares**.  Since every tile must contain at least\none of them (otherwise the tile would be completely surrounded by\nuncovered squares, which is impossible), any tiling of the board needs\nat least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Minimal number of tiles\n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with \\(