undefined}, &\\text{if }\\pi(i)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) defined \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(n-1\\) defined \\(r_i\\); all\n\\(2n-2\\) of them are distinct because the columns \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) (resp.\n\\(\\pi(i)+1\\)) are distinct for distinct \\(i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma  \n\nNo tile can contain two distinct distinguished squares (whether two\n\\(\\ell\\)\u2019s, two \\(r\\)\u2019s, or one \\(\\ell\\) and one \\(r\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a tile \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) contains two distinguished squares.\nConsider first two \\(\\ell\\)\u2019s: \\(\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and\n\\(\\ell_k=(k,\\pi(k)-1)\\) with \\(i\\neq k\\).  \nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, \\(\\pi(i)\\neq\\pi(k)\\); suppose\n\\(\\pi(i)<\\pi(k)\\).  \nSince both squares lie in \\(T\\), we have \\(c\\le\\pi(i)-1\\) and\n\\(d\\ge\\pi(k)-1\\ge\\pi(i)\\). Hence the column \\(\\pi(i)\\) lies between \\(c\\)\nand \\(d\\). The row interval \\([a,b]\\) contains \\(i\\); consequently the\nuncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\in U\\) would also belong to \\(T\\), contradicting\nthe definition of a tile.  \n\nThe same argument works for two \\(r\\)\u2019s (swap \u201cleft\u201d with \u201cright\u201d).  \n\nNow suppose \\(T\\) contains an \\(\\ell_i\\) and an \\(r_k\\).  \nIf \\(\\pi(i)\\le\\pi(k)\\), then \\(c\\le\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(d\\ge\\pi(k)+1\\ge\\pi(i)\\);\nagain column \\(\\pi(i)\\) lies inside \\([c,d]\\) and \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) would be\ncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \nIf \\(\\pi(i)>\\pi(k)\\), interchange the roles of the two rows and obtain the\nsame contradiction. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause a tile must intersect at least one row, it must contain the\ndistinguished square belonging to that row (the square immediately left or\nright of the uncovered one). By the lemma each tile can contain **at most\none** distinguished square. Therefore any tiling must use at least as many\ntiles as there are distinguished squares, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimal number of tiles  \n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles and proved that no\ntiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles equals\n\\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion:** Matilda can achieve the required condition with 4048 tiles,\nand it is impossible to do it with fewer. The minimum number of tiles is\n\\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
