on \\(p(i)=i\\) (any permutation works).  \n   * For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a rectangle covering rows \\(k,\\dots ,n\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,k-1\\). These \\(n-1\\) rectangles exactly fill the left region \\(L\\).  \n   * For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a rectangle covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) and columns \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\). These \\(n-1\\) rectangles exactly fill the right region \\(R\\).  \n   All rectangles avoid the uncovered squares \\((i,i)\\) and together cover every other unit square. The total number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n\n   With \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles.  \n\nSince the lower bound and the construction coincide, \\(4048\\) is the minimal possible number.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the condition must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares are covered by tiles.  \nEvery tile is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose interior consists solely of covered squares; therefore a tile cannot contain any point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Two disjoint regions\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) consists of the squares strictly to the left of the uncovered square in their row, and \\(R\\) consists of those strictly to the right.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus U = L\\cup R,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nIf a rectangle \\(T\\) avoids all points of \\(U\\), then for every row \\(i\\) intersected by \\(T\\) the whole column interval of \\(T\\) lies either entirely left of \\(p(i)\\) or entirely right of \\(p(i)\\). Consequently \\(T\\subseteq L\\) or \\(T\\subseteq R\\).  \nThus any tiling of the covered squares splits into a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the left region\n\nScan the rows from top to bottom and set  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\min\\{p(1),p(2),\\dots ,p(i)\\}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq L\\) that uses rows \\(r,\\dots ,s\\) (consecutive) we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\text{rightmost column of }T\\le m_{s}-1,\n\\]\n\nbecause every row among \\(r,\\dots ,s\\) contains an uncovered square at column at least \\(m_{s}\\).  \nConsequently, as soon as the running minimum \\(m_i\\) **decreases**, a rectangle that was covering rows up to \\(i-1\\) can no longer be extended to row \\(i\\); a new rectangle must start at row \\(i\\).\n\nSince \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the sequence \\((m_i)\\) starts at \\(p(1)\\) and drops exactly when a smaller value of the permutation appears. The values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) appear somewhere, so the minimum drops precisely \\(n-1\\) times. Therefore any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the right region\n\nDefine the running maximum  \n\n\\[\nM_i=\\max\\{p(1),p(2),\\dots ,p(i)\\}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nIf a rectangle \\(T\\subseteq R\\) uses rows \\(r,\\dots ,s\\), then its leftmost column must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\text{leftmost column of }T\\ge M_{s}+1,\n\\]\n\nbecause each of those rows has an uncovered square at column at most \\(M_{s}\\).  \nThus whenever the running maximum \\(M_i\\) **increases**, the current right\u2011hand rectangle cannot be prolonged to row \\(i\\); a new rectangle must begin.  \n\nAgain, because the permutation contains all numbers \\(1,\\dots ,n\\), the maximum increases exactly \\(n-1\\) times. Hence any tiling of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Global lower bound\n\nEvery tile belongs either to the tiling of \\(L\\) or to that of \\(R\\).  \nConsequently any admissible placement uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (any permutation works).  \nThe uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each \\(k=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{L}_{k}= \\{(i,j)\\mid k\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{L}_{k}\\) has height \\(n-k+1\\) and width \\(k-1\\); it lies completely in \\(L\\) because for every row \\(i\\ge k\\) we have \\(j\\le k-1 < i = p(i)\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T^{L}_{k}\\}_{k=2}^{n}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles whose union is exactly \\(L\\).\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{R}_{k}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any row \\(i\\le k\\) we have \\(j\\ge k+1 > i = p(i)\\), so \\(T^{R}_{k}\\subseteq R\\).  \nThe \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{T^{R}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are disjoint and together cover \\(R\\).\n\nAll uncovered squares \\((i,i)\\) are avoided, every other unit square belongs to exactly one of the constructed rectangles, and the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nPlugging \\(n=2025\\) into the bound gives  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nBoth the lower\u2011bound argument and the explicit tiling show that \\(4048\\) tiles are necessary and sufficient.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
