ence the minimum number of tiles equals\n   \\(2n-2\\). For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad n=2025 .\n\\]\n\nA **tile** is a set  \n\n\\[\nT=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\subseteq B,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a,b,c,d\\) are integers with \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and\n\\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nAll tiles are pairwise disjoint, their union together with a set \\(U\\) of\nuncovered unit squares equals \\(B\\), and each row and each column contains\nexactly one uncovered square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square, there exists a\nfunction \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe column condition forces \\(\\pi\\) to be bijective; thus \\(\\pi\\) is a\npermutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i=\\varnothing\\); if \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) then\n\\(R_i=\\varnothing\\).  \nWhenever non‑empty, \\(L_i\\) (resp. \\(R_i\\)) is a rectangle of height 1:\n\\[\nL_i=[i,i]\\times[1,\\pi(i)-1],\\qquad\nR_i=[i,i]\\times[\\pi(i)+1,n].\n\\]\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i = B\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\nExactly one of the sets \\(L_i\\) is empty (the row whose uncovered square lies\nin column 1) and exactly one of the sets \\(R_i\\) is empty (the row whose\nuncovered square lies in column \\(n\\)).  Consequently the number of\nnon‑empty rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{i\\mid\\pi(i)>1\\}\\bigr|+\\bigl|\\{i\\mid\\pi(i)<n\\}\\bigr|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a placement with \\(2n-2\\) tiles always exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor a tile \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) let  \n\n\\[\nR(T)=\\{i\\mid a\\le i\\le b\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of rows intersected by \\(T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1 (type of a rectangle)\n\nIf a tile \\(T\\) contains no uncovered square, then either  \n\n\\[\nd<\\min_{i\\in R(T)}\\pi(i)\\qquad\\text{(left‑type)}\n\\]\n\nor  \n\n\\[\nc>\\max_{i\\in R(T)}\\pi(i)\\qquad\\text{(right‑type)} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume the first inequality fails; then there exists \\(i\\in R(T)\\) with\n\\(\\pi(i)\\le d\\). If also \\(c\\le\\pi(i)\\), the uncovered square\n\\((i,\\pi(i))\\) would belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\nHence \\(c>\\pi(i)\\). Since this holds for some \\(i\\), we obtain\n\\(c>\\max_{i\\in R(T)}\\pi(i)\\), i.e. \\(T\\) is right‑type. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2 (injective assignment for left‑type rectangles)\n\nFor each left‑type rectangle \\(T\\) define  \n\n\\[\ni_T=\\operatorname*{arg\\,min}_{i\\in R(T)}\\pi(i).\n\\]\n\nThe map \\(T\\mapsto i_T\\) is injective and its image is contained in\n\\(\\{i\\mid\\pi(i)>1\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(T\\) is left‑type we have \\(d<\\pi(i_T)\\); consequently\n\\(\\pi(i_T)>1\\).  \nIf two distinct left‑type rectangles \\(T_1,T_2\\) had the same\n\\(i_T\\), both would contain squares of row \\(i_T\\) and would be confined to\ncolumns \\(\\le\\pi(i_T)-1\\). Hence they would overlap, contradicting the\ndisjointness of the tiles. Therefore the assignment is injective. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 3 (injective assignment for right‑type rectangles)\n\nFor each right‑type rectangle \\(T\\) define  \n\n\\[\ni_T=\\operatorname*{arg\\,max}_{i\\in R(T)}\\pi(i).\n\\]\n\nThe map \\(T\\mapsto i_T\\) is injective and its image lies in\n\\(\\{i\\mid\\pi(i)<n\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Lemma 2, using the condition\n\\(c>\\pi(i_T)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Counting argument\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) and exactly \\(n-1\\) rows\nwith \\(\\pi(i)<n\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 each left‑type rectangle is assigned a distinct row among the\nfirst set, so at least \\(n-1\\) left‑type rectangles are required.\nBy Lemma 3 at least \\(n-1\\) right‑type rectangles are required.\nA rectangle cannot be simultaneously left‑type and right‑type (otherwise its\ncolumn interval would be empty), therefore the total number of rectangles\nsatisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality\n\nStep 2 provides a tiling using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and Step 3 shows that\nfewer tiles are impossible. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the minimum number of rectangular tiles required is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
