uction of Step\u202f2 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and Step\u202f3 shows that\n   fewer tiles are impossible. Hence the minimum number of tiles equals\n   \\(2n-2\\). For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad n=2025 .\n\\]\n\nA **tile** is a set  \n\n\\[\nT=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\subseteq B,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a,b,c,d\\) are integers with \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and\n\\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nAll tiles are pairwise disjoint, their union together with a set \\(U\\) of\nuncovered unit squares equals \\(B\\), and each row and each column contains\nexactly one uncovered square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square, there exists a\nfunction \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe column condition forces \\(\\pi\\) to be bijective; thus \\(\\pi\\) is a\npermutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i=\\varnothing\\); if \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) then\n\\(R_i=\\varnothing\\).  \nWhenever non\u2011empty, \\(L_i\\) (resp. \\(R_i\\)) is a rectangle of height\u202f1:\n\\[\nL_i=[i,i]\\times[1,\\pi(i)-1],\\qquad\nR_i=[i,i]\\times[\\pi(i)+1,n].\n\\]\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i = B\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\nExactly one of the sets \\(L_i\\) is empty (the row whose uncovered square lies\nin column\u202